#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# quantity_array

#% text
# `quantity_array(v, unit)` gives the array `v` with every entry a quantity of `unit`; `quantity_array(v, [u1, ..., uk])` gives the entries at position `i` of each innermost list the unit `ui`. The result is a list of quantities, so list functions and quantity arithmetic apply to it directly.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
quantity_array([1, 2, 3], "Meters")
#% output
#= [quantity(1, "Meters"), quantity(2, "Meters"), quantity(3, "Meters")]

#% cell
quantity_array([[1, 2], [3, 4]], ["Meters", "Seconds"])
#% output
#= [[quantity(1, "Meters"), quantity(2, "Seconds")], [quantity(3, "Meters"), quantity(4, "Seconds")]]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# List functions work on the quantities:

#% cell
total(quantity_array([1, 2, 3], "Meters"))
#% output
#= quantity(6, "Meters")

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `quantity`, `quantity_magnitude`
